"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2 stage, positive surgical margins, and absence of seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis. The biopsy revealed a higher grade (Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 3) with a cribriform pattern, which contrasts with the lower grade noted in the prostatectomy specimen. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the preoperative high PSA of 9.3. The nodal status was documented as negative, which is reassuring. The discrepancy between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings introduces uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty surrounding the trajectory of the disease. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 50.23–75.35 months; its coverage has not been validated."
